8:31  error  Parameter should be a read only type  @typescript-eslint/prefer-readonly-parameter-types
```

That's what 8.57.1 does.

### Actual Result

Only the interface gets reported. The type alias is treated as allowed:

```
8:31  error  Parameter should be a read only type  @typescript-eslint/prefer-readonly-parameter-types
```

### Additional Info

Seeing this on 8.71.1 with TS 6.0.3. It started in 8.57.2 with #11954. The alias check that PR added to `isTypeReadonlyRecurser` ([isTypeReadonly.ts#L234-L249](https://github.com/typescript-eslint/typescript-eslint/blob/8ba19195bf7967dfd7cfdbaf67f294075dcc0d96/packages/type-utils/src/isTypeReadonly.ts#L234-L249)) only compares the alias name with the names in `allow`, so `from`, `path` and `package` never get looked at. Any entry with a matching name allows a type alias no matter where it's declared. Same thing with `{ from: "file", name: "OptionsAlias", path: "other.ts" }` or `{ from: "lib", name: "OptionsAlias" }`. Interfaces still match correctly. I'd expect aliases to go through the same from/path/package matching as `typeMatchesSpecifier`.

Related, and probably the same code: an alias of a mapped type still can't be allowed by its own name. With `type Options = Partial<Settings>` and `{ from: "file", name: "Options" }` the parameter is still reported, because TypeScript keeps `Partial` as the alias symbol ([playground](https://typescript-eslint.io/play/#ts=6.0.3&fileType=.ts&code=JYOwLgpgTgZghgYwgAgMoTGUBzAzsgbwChllhIBbXALmVzChwG0BdAbiIF8iwBPABxQB5flgD2IfAF5kABThQscADYAedJhy4AfByIB6fcgCCy5WIDuEACbIARr2Qg4FCABp7AVzB0sZ5FAQ-GKKNrRgABYofIIA5PgqwHC4qLwUdmLKZPgABvKKSco5HiBiPjki4pI5AHREEAAewYrIMJ4gCFXICBIwwNiegQAUYqLAEjTIleOSAJS0AG5iwLbEpD2SmRA15tgjYxOzHNxAA&eslintrc=N4KABGBEBOCuA2BTAzpAXGUEKQAIBcBPABxQGNoBLY-AWhXkoDt8B6Y6RAM0Wls4CGAEwD2TeIVrEB0AQFtE+XrSKlUGANrhsOXtBHRIAGm07gUAfHgiA7ujAbzkLvrn3nlJMahN5idwDyNJRiqGAAvgC6EaZgkdrhIIlAA&tsconfig=N4KABGBEDGD2C2AHAlgGwKYCcDyiAuysAdgM6QBcYoEEkJemy0eFYDAruuGAL4g9A&tokens=false)). Happy to open a separate issue for that if you'd rather track it on its own.

